<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · 1895 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Queen's Prayer</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="haw">Ke Aloha O Ka Haku</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn that Queen Liliʻuokalani of Hawaiʻi wrote in 1895, while she was kept a prisoner in her own palace.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Liliʻuokalani</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Liliʻuokalani</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Native Hawaiians; the Kingdom of Hawaiʻi</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>ʻIolani Palace, Hawaiʻi</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>22 March 1895</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Liliʻuokalani (1838–1917), composed 1895)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> (1838–1917), the last queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a>, was one of Hawaiʻi's most accomplished composers and musicians; she wrote more than 165 songs and chants. &quot;The Queen's Prayer&quot;, in Hawaiian &quot;Ke Aloha O Ka Haku&quot;, is a mele she composed on 22 March 1895 while she was held under house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a>, and dedicated to her niece and heir apparent, Victoria Kaʻiulani. At the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> she wrote: &quot;Composed during my imprisonment at ʻIolani Palace by the Missionary party who overthrew my government.&quot; She meant the overthrow of the Hawaiian monarchy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> business interests, backed by the United States government. The hymn was published as &quot;Liliʻuokalani's Prayer&quot;, with its Hawaiian title and an English translation, &quot;The Lord's Mercy&quot;; it is now commonly called &quot;The Queen's Prayer&quot;.</p><p>It was one of several songs of her imprisonment. &quot;Kuʻu Pua I Paoakalani&quot; was written for John Wilson, a young haole supporter whose mother, Evelyn T. Wilson, went into voluntary imprisonment with the Queen: he regularly brought her flowers from her garden at Paoakalani wrapped in newspaper, and through the newspapers she could read the news that was kept from her. She set Psalms 121 and 126 in hymns dated June 1895, and in &quot;Ka Wai ʻApo Lani&quot; expressed the hope that she would be returned to the throne.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer of a captive queen. She praises the Lord's love as high as the heavens and his truth as perfect, speaks of her own sorrow and imprisonment, and calls him her light and support; she asks him not to look in anger on the sins of men but to forgive and cleanse them, and to keep her people in peace beneath his wings for ever. The mood is quiet, grieving and forgiving.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hawaiian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hawaiian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻO kou aloha nō</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your loving mercy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Aia i ka lani</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is as high as Heaven</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A ʻo Kou ʻoia ʻiʻo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And your truth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">He hemolele hoʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So perfect</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Koʻu noho mihi ʻana</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I live in sorrow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A paʻahao ʻia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Imprisoned</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻO ʻoe kuʻu lama</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are my light</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kou nani koʻu koʻo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your glory, my support</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Mai nānā ʻinoʻino</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold not with malevolence</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā hewa o kānaka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sins of man</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Akā e huikala</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But forgive</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A maʻemaʻe nō</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And cleanse</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">No laila e ka Haku</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And so, o Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ma lalo o kou ʻēheu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Protect us beneath your wings</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kō mākou maluhia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And let peace be our portion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A mau loa aku nō</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now and forever more</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Āmene</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Amen</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Liliʻuokalani (1895); the Hawaiian text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_compositions_by_Lili%CA%BBuokalani" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English translation from Wikipedia's article (CC BY-SA). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_compositions_by_Lili%CA%BBuokalani" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
